---
// The explanation below the Lab: the field, the symbols, a network instead
// of a table, replay and the target network, ε, and the model in short.
// Static HTML; formulas are rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import type { Dataset } from './data';

import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

interface Props {
  dataset: Dataset;
}

const { dataset } = Astro.props;
const { settings } = dataset;
const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="dqn-data">
    <h2 id="dqn-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      A mouse starts in the bottom-left square of a small field. The cheese is
      worth +10, a cat costs 10, and every step costs 1, so a short safe path is
      best. This is the same game as Q-Learning, but the mouse's knowledge is
      kept in a small neural network instead of a table.
    </p>
    <p>
      The triangles show the network's value for each move from each square:
      green good, orange bad. The arrows are its best moves, and the dashed line
      is the path it would take. The pink arrows are the moves it just practised
      from memory. Below, the chart shows how much reward each episode earned.
      The fields are made up, and <strong>Refresh Data</strong> shows another.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dqn-symbols">
    <h2 id="dqn-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>s, a, r, s′</dt>
        <dd>A square, a move, its reward, and the square it led to.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Q(s, a)</dt>
        <dd>
          The network's value for that move: the reward it expects from here on.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">y</dt>
        <dd>
          The target: the reward plus γ times the best value of the next square.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>γ</dt>
        <dd>How much later rewards count: here {settings.gamma}.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-error">L</dt>
        <dd>
          The loss: how far the network's value is from the target, squared.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-a">ε</dt>
        <dd>
          How often the mouse tries a random move instead of its best one.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dqn-network">
    <h2 id="dqn-network">A network instead of a table</h2>
    <p>
      Q-Learning keeps one number for every square and move. That works for a
      small grid, but not when there are millions of possible situations, like
      the screens of a video game. A Deep Q-Network replaces the table with a
      neural network: it takes a description of the situation and gives a value
      for every move.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.network} />
    <p>
      Here the description is just the square's column and row, and the network
      has {settings.hidden} hidden units. Because nearby squares have similar
      inputs, what the mouse learns in one square spreads to its neighbours,
      even ones it has never visited. That is the strength of a network, and
      also its risk: a lesson can spread where it does not belong.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dqn-learn">
    <h2 id="dqn-learn">How it learns</h2>
    <p>
      Learning works like Q-Learning: a move's value should equal its reward
      plus the discounted value of the next square. The network is nudged, by
      gradient descent, to shrink the gap.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.loss} />
    <p>
      Two tricks keep this steady. <strong>Replay:</strong> every move goes into
      a memory of the last {settings.capacity}, and each step the network
      practises on {settings.batch} moves drawn from it at random, not just on
      the latest move. That breaks up runs of similar moves, which would
      otherwise pull the network one way and then another.{' '}
      <strong>A target network:</strong> the targets y come from a copy of the
      network that is refreshed only every {settings.targetEvery} steps, so the
      network is not chasing a target that moves with every update.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dqn-explore">
    <h2 id="dqn-explore">Exploring, and the reward chart</h2>
    <p>
      With chance ε the mouse tries a random move, so it keeps discovering new
      squares. Early episodes are long and costly; as the network improves, the
      reward per episode climbs towards the best possible. The chart is bumpy
      because random moves sometimes walk the mouse into a cat.
    </p>
    <p>
      Training is not guaranteed to succeed. Change ε and the whole run plays
      out differently: sometimes the network finds the shortest path, sometimes
      it settles on a longer one or none at all. Deep reinforcement learning is
      famous for this.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dqn-summary">
    <h2 id="dqn-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Act:</strong> take the network's best move, or with chance ε a
        random one, and remember it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recall</strong> a few remembered moves at random.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Aim</strong> each at its target y, using the slow-changing
        target network.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nudge</strong> the network's weights to shrink the squared gap,
        and repeat.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }
</style>
